A||N||7||Monkeyman||DOOR LOCK PROBLEMS||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||05-28-2000||09:41 AM||||My passenger door lock keeps giving me trouble.  It seems to be jamming up, but I've removed the inner door panel and it seemed to work fine.  I can't see where anything on the door panel would cause it to bind.  I greased it, too.  Just can't unlock it.  Seems like (even with the door panel off) it would want to start to lock everytime I closed the door.  Does this make any sense?  I looked and felt inside the door at the locking mechanism and couldn't see anything that was out of the ordinary (although I'm not 100% sure what to look for).  I really don't want to remove the outer panel, but what else should I do??????||||reg||1
Z||000001||Coconut||05-28-2000||02:11 PM||||Monkeyman, I'm not real clear if your problem is with the doorlatch or the actual doorlock cylinder itself. From the way I'm reading your post, it sounds as if your problem is with the doorlatch? I'm assuming that when you took the inner panel off, you tried playing with the door release handle while looking to see if the latch is operating properly (i.e. latch opening & closing) If that seems to be working properly then I would think that it would either be your lock cylinder or (shot in the dark) a possible door alignment problem? Hey guys, how about some other input, it's scary when I gotta try to help with a tech question. I'm not the best troubleshooter in the world. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I'm sure there are others that can steer you right Monkeyman, I'm just trying to help with what limited knowledge I have.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spyder||05-28-2000||03:22 PM||||Ok, I’ll take a shot at it. <BR>But first a couple of questions.<BR>Power Locks or Manual<BR>Inside Lock or Outside Lock or both, other words does, i.e., the inside work and the outside doesn’t.<BR>Does the door handle itself work properly, outside and inside, when you lift up on the handle on the outside does it spring back down or free moving.<BR>The actual lock mechanism is at the latch itself.<BR>It sounds like something is loose.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ozzy||05-28-2000||03:30 PM||||MM...<P>I just changed the door lock cylinders on the '84 I'm tryin' to sell...It had the same problem, or at least a similar one; both door mechanisms would not lock/unlock completly by the electric system.<P>There are quite a few linkages that I could see. I.E. many points to bind up. I lubed them all. The lock cylinders would need this too. Mine were new and did not need lubed. The place that was the bigggest problem on mine was the main latch mechanism. I't was dirty and grimy and dry. I could not get to it any other way than to squirt WD-40 on it with the small tube attached. This freed up evrything enough to allow the elec. locks to work properly.<P>Hope this helps...<P>Ozzy<P>P.S. I assumed you are talking about power locks...If not, maybe the lock cylinder itself is worn...tumblers etc. I replaced mine 'cause someone tried to break into it. They are $20.00 for a set of 2 at the FS. Also a sh*t to change.<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 05-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||05-28-2000||06:05 PM||||Power locks, brand new cylinders (Ozzy--pass side was a breeze, but the drivers side... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">).  I'm pretty sure it's in the locking system, not the release system.  The door handles (both inside and outside) feel the same as the other door when it's locked.  I've tried to slide the manual door lock to no avail.  I've also tried using the key on the outside, but that didn't seem to be doing anything, either.  I do have a slight alignment problem, but I'm not sure what that would have to do with the locking mechanisms.  I can probably take the entire latch assy off of the Monkeymobile if I absolutely have to, but I really don't want to remove any outer door panels unless it's absolutely neccessary.  I"ve sprayed WD-40 on the assys.  (I got the door to unlock the other day.  Took the inner panel off and messed with it for a couple of hours.)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ozzy||05-28-2000||06:40 PM||||Hmmmm...Yeah those cylinders kept me cussin' for awhile dude. That retainer clip to hold the lock cyl. assy. in place was the tough part.<P>All I can think of is the latching assy. might be worn and stuck or something. If all of the linkages are in place correctly etc, there is not a lot else it could be. It's a reasonably simple setup. Just hard to get at thru the inside. <P>Just a thought...I remember keeping carefull track of the way the lever attaches to the rear of the new cylinders. Is it possible to have the lock cyl. upside down? the travel of the lock cyl. would probably not allow the mechanism to function properly.(??)<P>Just stabbing in the dark... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||05-28-2000||10:24 PM||||I'm fairly certain that the lock cylinders are oriented correctly (the key goes in the right way).  Should I change the entire latch assy?  It appears to be slightly loose (not the entire assy, just the "C" part that swivels around the bolt part).  If I have to change the entire assy, can I do it through the inside of the door or do I have to remove the outer panel?  (I'm really trying to avoid removing the outer panel. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||05-29-2000||12:45 PM||||Removed the bezel (?) around the door handle and the lock seems to work fine now.  I replaced the bezel minus the plastic part that clips on the lock rod.  (It still takes a couple of taps on the power switch but that's nothing unusual.)  I'll keep an eye on this for a few days and see if any more problems develop.  Assuming they don't, the problems must be something binding in the bezel?  I don't see what could be binding.||||reg||1
